  "theorem_statement": "The exact group W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q), equivalently W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21), is a direct sum of Q(8); a disproof must exhibit a surviving non-Tate summand in this exact group.",
  "theorem_scope": {"g": 6, "n": 6},
  "summary": "Proof. The exact target sits in ordinary cohomological degree 26, which is exactly the virtual cohomological dimension 4g-4+n = 26 of M_{6,6}. The whole group H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) vanishes: M_{6,6} is a rational classifying space for the pure mapping class group Mod_{6,6}, Harer's virtual duality gives vcd(Mod_{6,6}) = 26, and the Church-Farb-Putman top-degree vanishing theorem (g >= 2, n >= 1) gives H^{4g-4+n}(Mod_{g,n};Q) = 0. Poincare duality on the smooth 21-dimensional Deligne-Mumford stack M_{6,6} (realised via a level-l cover and finite-group invariants, so that the identification is one of mixed Hodge structures) transports this to H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) = H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21) = 0. Hence W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0, which is the direct sum of the empty family of copies of Q(8); in particular no summand at all, and a fortiori no non-Tate summand, survives in the exact group. The tetragonal Casnati-Ekedahl evaluation-failure apparatus of the packet is not needed and is shown to be consistent with (not contradicted by) this outcome. The proof does not use vanishing strictly above the virtual cohomological dimension; it uses vanishing exactly at the virtual cohomological dimension, which is declared as new input.",
  "argument_markdown": "# Resolution of C66-001: the exact group vanishes, hence is a direct sum of Q(8)\n\n## 0. Exact theorem and outcome\n\n**Exact theorem.** *The exact group `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q)`, equivalently `W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21)`, is a direct sum of Q(8); a disproof must exhibit a surviving non-Tate summand in this exact group.*\n\n**Outcome: the theorem is true.** We prove the sharp form\n\n> `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0`,\n\nand `0` is the direct sum of the empty family of copies of `Q(8)`. In particular the exact group has **no** nonzero direct summand whatsoever, so in particular no surviving non-Tate summand; the disproof clause is therefore unsatisfiable.\n\n**Reading of the phrase 'is a direct sum of Q(8)'.** A Q-mixed Hodge structure `V` *is a direct sum of Q(8)* means `V` is isomorphic to `⊕_{i∈I} Q(8)` for some index set `I`. The case `I = ∅` gives `V = 0`. This is the standard reading, and it is exactly the reading forced by the packet's own dichotomy: the packet states that the theorem is falsified precisely by exhibiting *a surviving non-Tate summand in this exact group*. The zero object has no nonzero summand, hence no non-Tate summand, hence lies on the Tate side of the packet's dichotomy. (Nothing in the packet asserts that the exact group is nonzero: `FND-0005` asserts nonemptiness of a geometric locus `Z ⊂ M_{6,6}`, which is a statement about a codimension-4 subvariety and carries no implication about the cohomology of `M_{6,6}` in degree 26; see §6.3.)\n\nThroughout, all (co)homology has `Q`-coefficients; `Q(m)` is the Tate Hodge structure of weight `-2m`; `M_{g,n}` is the moduli stack of smooth `n`-pointed genus-`g` curves with **ordered** distinct markings, a smooth separated Deligne-Mumford stack over `C` of pure dimension `d = 3g-3+n`; `W_\\bullet` is Deligne's weight filtration.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Numerical setup\n\nFor `(g,n) = (6,6)`:\n\n- `d := dim_C M_{6,6} = 3g-3+n = 18-3+6 = 21`.\n- `2d-16 = 42-16 = 26`.\n- `vcd(M_{6,6}) = 4g-4+n = 24-4+6 = 26`.\n- Tate-twist bookkeeping: `Q(-13)(21) = Q(-13+21) = Q(8)`.\n\nThese are the packet's own target data (`dimension 21`, `ordinary_cohomology_degree 26`, `poincare_twist 21`, `ordinary_tate_type Q(-13)`, `open_bm_tate_type Q(8)`), re-derived here by direct substitution.\n\n**The decisive numerical coincidence.** The ordinary degree attached to the target, `2d-16 = 26`, is *equal* to `vcd(M_{6,6}) = 4g-4+n = 26`. The packet's `FND-0035` records exactly this and correctly observes that vanishing *strictly above* the virtual cohomological dimension therefore proves nothing here. The present proof does **not** use vanishing strictly above vcd. It uses vanishing **exactly at** vcd, which is a strictly deeper and logically independent statement (it fails, for instance, for `g = 1`: `vcd(M_{1,11}) = 11` and `H^{11}(M_{1,11};Q) ≠ 0`). See §7 for the audit of this point against the packet's blocked routes.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Step 1: Poincaré duality for `M_{6,6}` as an identification of mixed Hodge structures\n\nThis step re-derives, in the exact case at hand and without appeal to the adjudicated finding, the identification recorded as `FND-0001`.\n\n**Lemma 2.1 (global quotient presentation by a smooth quasi-projective variety).** Fix an integer `l ≥ 3`. Let `M_{6,6}[l]` denote the moduli space of smooth 6-pointed genus-6 curves equipped with a full symplectic level-`l` structure on `H_1(C;Z/l)`. Then `M_{6,6}[l]` is a smooth quasi-projective complex variety of dimension 21, the forgetful morphism `M_{6,6}[l] → M_{6,6}` is finite étale and Galois with deck group `G := Sp(12, Z/l)`, and `M_{6,6} ≅ [ M_{6,6}[l] / G ]`.\n\n*Proof.* By Serre's lemma, a nontrivial automorphism of a smooth projective curve of genus `g ≥ 1` acts nontrivially on `H_1(C;Z/l)` for `l ≥ 3`. Hence a 6-pointed genus-6 curve with level-`l` structure has no nontrivial automorphisms, so the level-`l` moduli stack is representable by a scheme; it is smooth because `M_{6,6}` is smooth and the level cover is étale, and quasi-projective by standard GIT/Hilbert-scheme constructions of level moduli. The level-`l` structures on a fixed curve form a torsor under `Sp(12,Z/l)`, whence the finite étale Galois property and the quotient presentation. ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.2 (invariants).** For every `k`, `H^k(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^k(M_{6,6}[l];Q)^{G}` and `H^{BM}_k(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^{BM}_k(M_{6,6}[l];Q)^{G}`, both as mixed Hodge structures.\n\n*Proof.* For a finite group `G` acting on a variety `Y`, the (co)homology of the quotient stack `[Y/G]` with `Q`-coefficients is the `G`-invariant part of that of `Y`; this is the standard Leray/descent computation for the finite étale Galois cover `Y → [Y/G]`, valid with `Q`-coefficients because `|G|` is invertible. The `G`-action is by algebraic automorphisms, hence acts by morphisms of mixed Hodge structures, and taking `G`-invariants is an exact functor on `Q`-mixed Hodge structures (it is a direct summand cut out by the idempotent `|G|^{-1} Σ_{g∈G} g`), so it commutes with `W_\\bullet` and preserves the mixed Hodge structure. ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.3 (Poincaré duality).** `H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21)` as mixed Hodge structures, and consequently\n\n`W_{-16} H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ ( W_{26} H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) )(21)`.\n\n*Proof.* Let `Y := M_{6,6}[l]`, a smooth complex variety of pure dimension `d = 21`. Poincaré duality for a smooth complex variety gives a natural isomorphism of mixed Hodge structures `H^{BM}_k(Y;Q) ≅ H^{2d-k}(Y;Q)(d)` (Deligne; the duality between Borel-Moore homology and cohomology of a smooth oriented manifold, upgraded to mixed Hodge structures by the standard functoriality of Deligne's construction). It is natural, hence `G`-equivariant. Taking `G`-invariants and applying Lemma 2.2 on both sides gives `H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21)`. Since the weight filtration is functorial and `V ↦ V(21)` shifts weights by `-42`, the weight-`(-16)` part on the left corresponds to the weight-`(-16+42) = 26` part on the right, giving the displayed consequence. ∎\n\nLemma 2.3 is precisely the packet's exact-target identification `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6}) = W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6})(21)`, now proved rather than assumed. It also verifies the twist dictionary: a summand `Q(-13)` of `W_{26}H^{26}` corresponds to `Q(-13)(21) = Q(8)` in the Borel-Moore normalisation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Step 2: `M_{6,6}` is a rational classifying space for the pure mapping class group\n\n**Lemma 3.1.** For every `k` there is an isomorphism of `Q`-vector spaces `H^k(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^k(Mod_{6,6};Q)`, where `Mod_{6,6}` is the pure mapping class group of a genus-6 orientable surface with 6 punctures (orientation-preserving mapping classes fixing each puncture).\n\n*Proof.* The Teichmüller space `T_{6,6}` is a contractible complex manifold of complex dimension 21, `Mod_{6,6}` acts on it properly discontinuously with finite stabilisers, and the analytification of the stack `M_{6,6}` is the quotient orbifold `[T_{6,6}/Mod_{6,6}]`. For a group `Γ` acting properly discontinuously with finite stabilisers on a contractible space `X`, the natural map `H^*(Γ;Q) → H^*(X/Γ;Q)` is an isomorphism (the Borel construction `X ×_Γ EΓ → X/Γ` has `Q`-acyclic fibres `BΓ_x` for finite `Γ_x`, and `X ×_Γ EΓ ≃ BΓ` since `X` is contractible). Finally the comparison `H^*(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^*(M_{6,6}^{an};Q)` holds for the smooth Deligne-Mumford stack `M_{6,6}`. ∎\n\n(Only the underlying `Q`-vector-space statement is used below; no Hodge-theoretic content is claimed for Lemma 3.1.)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Step 3: virtual cohomological dimension\n\n**Theorem 4.1 (Harer).** For `g ≥ 1` and `n ≥ 1`, `Mod_{g,n}` is a virtual duality group of dimension `4g-4+n`; in particular `vcd(Mod_{g,n}) = 4g-4+n` and `H^k(Mod_{g,n};Q) = 0` for `k > 4g-4+n`. (For `g ≥ 2`, `n = 0`, the value is `4g-5`.)\n\nThis is Harer's theorem on the virtual cohomological dimension of the mapping class group, obtained from the action of `Mod_{g,n}` on the (contractible) arc complex, whose quotient by the non-filling part yields a spine of dimension `4g-4+n`, together with the virtual duality statement whose dualising module is the Steinberg module `St_{g,n}` (the top reduced homology of the subcomplex of non-filling arc systems).\n\n**Corollary 4.2.** `vcd(Mod_{6,6}) = 4·6-4+6 = 26`, so `H^k(M_{6,6};Q) = 0` for `k > 26`, and `26` is the top degree in which `H^*(M_{6,6};Q)` can be nonzero.\n\nBecause the exact target lives in degree `26 = vcd`, Corollary 4.2 alone is inert — this is precisely the content of `FND-0035`. Step 4 supplies the extra input.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Step 4: vanishing exactly in the virtual cohomological dimension\n\n**Theorem 5.1 (Church-Farb-Putman).** Let `g ≥ 2`. Then\n\n- `H^{4g-4+n}(Mod_{g,n};Q) = 0` for every `n ≥ 1`, and\n- `H^{4g-5}(Mod_{g};Q) = 0`.\n\nThat is, the rational cohomology of the mapping class group vanishes in its virtual cohomological dimension.\n\n*Source and status.* T. Church, B. Farb, A. Putman, *The rational cohomology of the mapping class group vanishes in its virtual cohomological dimension*, Int. Math. Res. Not. IMRN **2012**, no. 21, 5025-5030. (The unpunctured case `n = 0` was obtained independently by Morita-Sakasai-Suzuki.) This is a published theorem, cited here as an external input; it is declared explicitly in `new_inputs` as the genuinely new input relative to the packet's context revision 2, in which no top-vcd vanishing theorem was asserted (`FND-0035`: 'no arbitrary-puncture top-vcd vanishing theorem is asserted').\n\n*Structure of the input, for auditability.* By Harer's virtual duality (Theorem 4.1) with dualising module the Steinberg module `St_{g,n}`, one has `H^{vcd}(Mod_{g,n};Q) ≅ H_0(Mod_{g,n}; St_{g,n} ⊗ Q) = (St_{g,n} ⊗ Q)_{Mod_{g,n}}`, the module of coinvariants. Theorem 5.1 is the statement that these rational coinvariants vanish; the proof proceeds by exhibiting a generating set of `St_{g,n}` consisting of classes each of which is negated by some element of the mapping class group, so that each generator dies in the rational coinvariants.\n\n*Sharpness / non-triviality of the hypothesis `g ≥ 2`.* The hypothesis is essential and marks the theorem as strictly stronger than any 'degree exceeds vcd' vanishing: for `g = 1` one has `vcd(Mod_{1,11}) = 4·1-4+11 = 11` and `H^{11}(M_{1,11};Q) ≠ 0` (it carries the weight-11, non-Tate motive attached to the cusp form of weight 12). Thus the top-degree vanishing is a genuine theorem about genus at least two and not a formal consequence of the dimension bound.\n\n**Corollary 5.2.** `H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0`.\n\n*Proof.* Apply Theorem 5.1 with `g = 6 ≥ 2` and `n = 6 ≥ 1`: `4g-4+n = 26`, so `H^{26}(Mod_{6,6};Q) = 0`. By Lemma 3.1, `H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^{26}(Mod_{6,6};Q) = 0`. ∎\n\n---\n\n## 6. Step 5: conclusion, and consistency with the packet\n\n### 6.1 The exact group\n\n**Theorem 6.1.** `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0`. Equivalently, `W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21) = 0`. In particular the exact group is the direct sum `⊕_{i∈∅} Q(8)` of the empty family of copies of `Q(8)`, and it possesses no nonzero direct summand; a fortiori it possesses no surviving non-Tate summand.\n\n*Proof.* By Corollary 5.2, `H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0`. Every subquotient of the zero vector space is zero, so `W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0` and hence `W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21) = 0`. By Lemma 2.3, `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ (W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q))(21) = 0`. (Alternatively and even more directly: by Lemma 2.3 the whole group `H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)(21)` vanishes, hence so does any weight-graded piece of it.)\n\nA direct sum decomposition `0 = ⊕_{i∈I} V_i` in the abelian category of `Q`-mixed Hodge structures forces `V_i = 0` for all `i`; therefore the exact group has no nonzero summand of any kind, Tate or non-Tate. Taking `I = ∅` and `V_i = Q(8)` exhibits the exact group as a direct sum of copies of `Q(8)`, which is the assertion of the exact theorem. ∎\n\nThis resolves the exact target `RED-0001` for `(g,n) = (6,6)` in the affirmative, i.e. as a **proof**, not a disproof: the disproof clause requires exhibiting a surviving non-Tate summand in this exact group, and Theorem 6.1 shows no such summand can exist.\n\n### 6.2 Consistency with `FND-0036` (and a corollary)\n\n`FND-0036` records that for the forgetful map `p : M_{6,6} → M_{6,5}` the term `H^{26}(M_{6,5};Q)` vanishes (since `26 > 25 = vcd(M_{6,5}) = 4·6-4+5`) and that, fibre cohomology being concentrated in `q = 0,1`, no Leray differential enters or leaves total degree 26. Consequently `H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) ≅ H^{25}(M_{6,5}; R^1p_*Q)`. Our Corollary 5.2 is therefore *equivalent*, along this route, to `H^{25}(M_{6,5}; R^1p_*Q) = 0`, which is what Theorem 5.1 delivers after transport. There is no conflict: `FND-0036` is a reduction, and Theorem 5.1 is the input that closes it. (Note also that the constant-coefficient statement `H^{25}(M_{6,5};Q) = 0`, i.e. Theorem 5.1 at `(6,5)`, does *not* by itself close the reduction, since the surviving Leray term carries the rank-16 coefficient system `R^1p_*Q`; this is why the input is applied at `(6,6)` directly.)\n\n### 6.3 Consistency with `FND-0005` and the tetragonal/Casnati-Ekedahl bottleneck\n\nThe packet's canonical geometric bottleneck constructs an evaluation-failure locus `Z ⊆ M_{6,6}` from the balanced tetragonal splitting `F = O_{P^1}(4) ⊕ O_{P^1}(5)` and the `f_2 = 5` Casnati-Ekedahl summand `W_5 := (O_{P(E^∨)}(2) ⊗ γ^*O_{P^1}(-5))|_C`, with the six-point failure condition `O_C(Γ) ≅ W_5 ⊗ ω_C^{-1}`. The degree arithmetic of `SRC-0004` Lemma 10.6 is correct and we reproduce it: `deg(W_f|_C) = 4g+12-4f`, so for `g = 6`, `deg(W_5) = 4·6+12-20 = 16` and `deg(W_5 ⊗ ω_C^{-1}) = 16-(2·6-2) = 6`; and `deg(ω_C ⊗ W_f^∨(Γ)) = 4f+n-2g-14` equals `-4` for `f = 4` and `0` for `f = 5` when `n = 6, g = 6`. Hence the `f = 4` obstruction vanishes identically and the `f = 5` obstruction is nonzero exactly when the degree-zero bundle `ω_C ⊗ W_5^∨(Γ)` is trivial, i.e. exactly when `O_C(Γ) ≅ W_5 ⊗ ω_C^{-1}`. In particular `FND-0005` (nonemptiness of `Z`) is entirely compatible with our result.\n\nThe key point is that **nonemptiness of a positive-codimension locus `Z ⊂ M_{6,6}` implies nothing about `H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)`**. All of the packet's subproblems `C66-GEO-Z`, `C66-GEO-H0`, `C66-GEO-COMP`, `C66-TATE-SUPPORT`, `C66-TATE-ASSEMBLY`, `C66-CEX-LOCAL`, `C66-CEX-SURVIVE` are strategies for *computing or bounding* a group that we have now shown to be zero. They are therefore unnecessary for the exact target, and their unresolved dependencies (comparison of determinantal/Fitting ideals with the Abel-Jacobi incidence scheme, the `h^0`-stratification of `|W_5 ⊗ ω_C^{-1}|`, control of Hurwitz monodromy, Gysin/localisation bookkeeping, weight-zero finite-monodromy local systems) are not dependencies of the present proof. In particular, the counterexample lanes `C66-CEX-LOCAL` and `C66-CEX-SURVIVE` are closed: even if a non-Tate local system exists on some stratum of `Z` (a possibility the packet correctly refuses to exclude), it cannot survive into a zero group.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Audit: inputs used, inputs not used, blocked routes\n\n**Inputs used.**\n\n1. Serre's lemma and the existence of smooth quasi-projective level-`l` moduli with finite Galois group (Lemma 2.1) — standard.\n2. Poincaré duality for smooth complex varieties as an isomorphism of mixed Hodge structures, plus exactness of finite-group invariants over `Q` (Lemmas 2.2, 2.3) — standard.\n3. Contractibility of Teichmüller space and the rational-classifying-space property (Lemma 3.1) — standard.\n4. Harer's virtual duality and `vcd(Mod_{g,n}) = 4g-4+n` for `n ≥ 1` (Theorem 4.1) — published.\n5. Church-Farb-Putman top-degree vanishing for `g ≥ 2` (Theorem 5.1) — published; declared as new input.\n\nEvery one of these is a proved statement; none is conjectural, none is conditional, and none is a candidate idea imported unproved from the working context. All statements taken from the working contexts that are used at all (the Poincaré-duality dictionary, the numerical values `d = 21`, `2d-16 = 26`, the Casnati-Ekedahl degree arithmetic quoted in §6.3) are re-derived here from first principles or from the primary-source excerpt.\n\n**Blocked routes: compliance.**\n\n- `vcd-only-vanishing`. The blocked route is the inference 'degree exceeds vcd, therefore the group vanishes'. That inference is *unavailable* here and we do not use it: `26 = vcd(M_{6,6})` exactly, as `FND-0035` records. What we use instead is vanishing **at** vcd (Theorem 5.1), a strictly stronger and logically independent theorem — as shown by the `g = 1`, `n = 11` counterexample in §5, where vanishing above vcd holds but vanishing at vcd fails. Insofar as this route is considered adjacent to the blocked one, the required *genuinely new input* is declared explicitly (Theorem 5.1, `NEWIN-01`), consistent with `FND-0035`'s statement that 'no arbitrary-puncture top-vcd vanishing theorem is asserted' in the packet.\n- `undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex`. Not used. We make no appeal to tropical moduli, `Δ_{g,n}`, or graph complexes. (Consistency check only, not used in the proof: the top-weight piece `Gr^W_{42}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)` would be `\\tilde H_{25}(Δ_{6,6};Q)`, which vanishes because `dim Δ_{6,6} = 3g-4+n = 20 < 25`; this is compatible with, and weaker than, Corollary 5.2.)\n- `point-count-without-degree-parity-separation`. Not used. No point counts, no finite-field computations, and no experiments of any kind enter this proof (`experiment_ids` is empty).\n- `forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown`. Not used. We do not close the argument by renaming `H^{26}(M_{6,6})` as `H^{25}(M_{6,5};R^1p_*Q)` and leaving the latter unknown; §6.2 is a consistency remark only, and the actual vanishing is obtained directly at `(6,6)`.\n\n**Fallacious inferences flagged in the working contexts: none is used.** We do not claim `M_{0,b}` has only even cohomology; we do not propagate pure-Tateness along finite étale covers; we do not deduce Tate type from rationality of `S_6`-representations or from finiteness over `M_{0,b}`; we do not assert an `r = 0` stratum for a degree-six bundle with `χ = 1`; we do not assume non-Tate variations have positive weight; we do not assume a non-Tate local system automatically survives a Gysin map; we do not infer from the vanishing of the `f = 4` obstruction that the `f = 5` condition is the unique global source of the target cohomology; we do not identify determinantal and Abel-Jacobi scheme structures from agreement on geometric points; we do not treat the relative linear system as `Proj(π_*L)` when `h^0` jumps; we do not treat the reduced-support open locus as closed; we do not apply a smooth codimension-4 Gysin sequence to a singular stratified locus; and we do not claim the balanced tetragonal Hurwitz space is rational or Tate. None of these enter, because the geometric route is not used at all.\n\n**Execution boundary.** No finite computation is used to prove a universal claim; indeed no computation beyond the elementary arithmetic of §1 is used. Every proof dependency is listed above and in the structured fields.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Statement of the result in the packet's normalisations\n\n- Ordinary normalisation: `W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0`, a direct sum of `0` copies of `Q(-13)`.\n- Borel-Moore normalisation: `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6};Q) = 0`, a direct sum of `0` copies of `Q(8)`.\n- Compactly supported dual normalisation: `Gr^W_{16}H^{16}_c(M_{6,6};Q) = 0`, a direct sum of `0` copies of `Q(-8)`.\n\nIn every normalisation the exact group is Tate (vacuously, as the zero object), and no non-Tate summand survives. **The exact theorem of packet `C66-001-v3` is proved.**",
